msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:18
msgid "// We can get a unique identifier for the type.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:21
msgid "// We can check if our object is a particular type.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:24
msgid "// We can attempt to downcast to a concrete type.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:33
msgid "\"A string\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:39
msgid ""
"By default, trait objects cannot be downcasted to their concrete type. This "
"is because they do not have any runtime type information that would allow us "
"to determine what the concrete type is, and Rust won't allow us to blindly "
"cast to a type that may not be correct."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:44
msgid ""
"The `Any` trait allows us to downcast values back from dyn values into "
"concrete values by adding the necessary runtime type information."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:47
msgid ""
"This is an auto trait: like Send/Sync/Sized, it is automatically implemented "
"for any type that meets specific criteria."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:50
msgid ""
"The criteria for Any is that a type is `'static`. That is, the type does not "
"contain any non-`'static` lifetimes within it."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:53
msgid ""
"Any offers two related behaviors: downcasting, and runtime checking of types "
"being the same."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:56
msgid ""
"In the example above, we see the ability to downcast from `Any` into "
"`ThisImplementsAny` automatically."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:59
msgid ""
"We also see `Any::is` being used to check to see what type the value is."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:61
msgid ""
"`Any` does not implement reflection for a type, it just adds the runtime "
"type information necessary to safely determine the concrete type at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:64
msgid ""
"You can add downcasting support to your own traits by using `Any` as a "
"supertrait."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md:67
msgid ""
"This also works for generics! We can use `Any` as a trait bound in our "
"generic code, and then \"downcast\" to a concrete type. This is uncommon, "
"though, as generics already have full type information and access to all "
"functionality provided by traits."
msgstr ""
